<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19868373</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2126693</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE OCCURRENCE OF BACILLUS INFLUENZ&#x000c6; IN THE NORMAL THROAT</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Winchell</surname><given-names>Agnes I.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Stillman</surname><given-names>Ernest G.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x326c4d0N0x3293e38">From the Hospital of The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>31</day><month>10</month><year>1919</year></pub-date><volume>30</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>497</fpage><lpage>504</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>15</day><month>7</month><year>1919</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1919, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> 1. The frequency of occurrence of <italic>Baculus influenz&#x00153;</italic> in the throats of normal individuals has been as great in the period subsequent to the epidemic of influenza as it was during the period of the epidemic. 2. <italic>Bacillus influenz&#x00153;</italic> may persist in the throats of healthy carriers for a considerable period of time. 3. A considerable proportion of individuals not exposed during the epidemic has been found to carry <italic>Bacillus influenz&#x00153;</italic> in their throats. 4. Oleate hemoglobin agar has been found to be a very satisfactory selective medium for cultivation of <italic>Bacillus influenz&#x00153;</italic> from the throat. To give optimum results it should have a reaction of from pH 7.2 to 7.5. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>